  "query_preview": "..\nAs of 1221 PM Saturday...\n\nKEY MESSAGE 1... Showers are tapering to areas of drizzle/mist. \nAnother cool and cloudy afternoon. Stratus again expected tonight.\n\nThe latest radar trends indicate showers/storms over the SE part of \nNC and also offshore. Light showers remain over central NC with a \nlow overcast. Temperatures were still in the 65-70 range over most \nof the region at early afternoon after a morning of additional \nlocally heavy rainfall. Raleigh's rainfall was quickly nearing 10 \ninches for July after morning downpours. \n\nSatellite data indicated some clearing over the Blue Ridge in NW NC \nand SW VA. However, a light NE flow continued below 2k feet and this \nwill likely persist through the afternoon. Some partial clearing \nwill be possible in the lee of the mountains and in the SE. However, \nthe stratus is expected to lower again tonight with some areas of \nfog expected. Afternoon highs today were lowered by 5+ degrees into \nthe lower to mid 70s with the CAD. \n\nKEY MESSAGE 2... A more typical late July weather pattern returns \nfor much of the work week. A cold frontal passage Tuesday night \nbrings the best chance of rain, with the potential for a few strong \nto severe storms. \n\nAs the front continues to move further southward and high pressure \nmoves into the Mid-Atlantic, a more typical summertime pattern will \nreturn to central NC by Monday. Maximum temperatures each day during \nthe work week should generally be in the upper 80s to lower 90s, \nwith Tuesday being the warmest day, potentially hitting the mid 90s. \nLows each morning will dip into the upper 60s to low 70s. \n\nThe best chance of rain will be from a weak cold front moving \nthrough the region Tuesday night into Wednesday. Ahead of the front, \na band of showers and thunderstorms should move through the region. \nModerate instability along with 20-30 kts of 0-6 km shear could \nsupport a few organized storms capable of producing damaging wind \ngusts. However, a later frontal passage may allow for a limited \nsevere risk as the rain may occur after the peak of heating and \ntherefore instability in the region. Details should come into better \nagreement as we get closer and in the range of the higher resolution \nmodels. Otherwise, a few diurnally induced showers and thunderstorms \nmay be possible each afternoon.",
